      <!-- ENGLISH -->
      <div class="lang-block">
        <div class="lang-flag">🇺🇸 English</div>
        <p class="lecture-text">
          Ocean dynamics describes the forces that govern fluid motion at the scales of currents, gyres, and mesoscale eddies. The dominant balance in the large-scale ocean is <strong>geostrophic balance</strong>: the pressure gradient force (water flowing from high to low pressure) is exactly opposed by the <strong>Coriolis force</strong> (Earth's rotation deflecting moving water), producing steady currents that flow <em>along</em> rather than across pressure contours. When geostrophic balance holds, the <strong>Rossby number</strong> (Ro = U/fL) is much less than 1, meaning rotation dominates over inertia. Departures from geostrophic balance drive <strong>ageostrophic flows</strong> — the meanders, instabilities, and eddies that transfer energy across scales. Ocean gyres are large, persistent, wind-driven circulation systems bounded by continental margins; subtropical gyres rotate <strong>clockwise</strong> in the Northern Hemisphere (anticyclonic) due to the combined action of trade winds, westerlies, and Coriolis. <strong>Vorticity</strong> — the tendency for fluid to rotate — is a conserved quantity in the ocean; stretching a water column increases its spin, while compression slows it, producing the <strong>beta effect</strong> that drives westward intensification of boundary currents like the Gulf Stream and the Yucatán Current.
        </p>
